{
  "_id": "a9258218-9e8f-4667-9dde-e6c5ec921be9",
  "content": [
    {
      "_key": "3a3d8e03b042",
      "_type": "textBlock",
      "body": [
        {
          "_key": "0973d7a83302",
          "_type": "block",
          "children": [
            {
              "_key": "1ce53b4682be",
              "_type": "span",
              "marks": [],
              "text": "Aster House is a twelve-room hotel in a converted 1890s seed merchant’s on the Kent coast. We built an identity and booking experience that behaves like the building — quiet, warm, and slow to reveal itself."
            }
          ],
          "markDefs": [],
          "style": "normal"
        },
        {
          "_key": "93a41ec74017",
          "_type": "block",
          "children": [
            {
              "_key": "94bb286e79a2",
              "_type": "span",
              "marks": [],
              "text": "The owners wanted to avoid the visual language of boutique hotels: no brass, no script logos, no lifestyle stock. The brief was a place that felt inherited rather than styled."
            }
          ],
          "markDefs": [],
          "style": "normal"
        },
        {
          "_key": "b2fce86177a1",
          "_type": "block",
          "children": [
            {
              "_key": "ac3064018ea7",
              "_type": "span",
              "marks": [],
              "text": "The system is built from three materials — limewash tones, a single typeface used at extremes, and photography shot only in natural light between 3 and 6pm."
            }
          ],
          "markDefs": [],
          "style": "normal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_key": "a7e1f527db84",
      "_type": "fullWidthImage",
      "alt": "Window light falling on the reception wall",
      "caption": "Reception, 4.40pm. The light study that set the photographic brief.",
